fuxi: 0107 -- flush the console ring from panic(); make the USB probe say where it dies

Two blind spots, one patch.

1. Panics left no log at all.  The ring on userdata is written by a kernel thread
   every ufslogsecs; a panic halts every other thread and CPU, so the ring keeps
   whatever the *previous* boot flushed.  The sweethw=1 abort is a case in point:
   it kills the boot in the same second the log starts, ufslogsecs=1 included, so
   the ring still read 181267 bytes from the boot before it.  panic() now calls
   fuxi_log_flush_panic(), which writes the last 16 KiB of the ring through a
   slot reserved at log start and stamps the header seq 0xffffffff so a reader can
   tell a panic flush from a periodic one.

2. fuxi_usb_probe() died before its first print.  It now says each step
   ("probe start", "mapping DWC3", "mapping QUSB2 PHY", "reading GCC ...") so the
   ring names the access that faults, and the GCC clock/GDSC probe is behind
   sweetusbgcc (default 0) instead of being unconditional.

   Why the GCC probe is off by default: those CBCR/GDSC offsets are copied from
   gcc-sdmmagpie.c, which is SM7150, not SM8550.  An offset the SoC does not
   implement answers with an AXI decode error, and that is a *synchronous
   external abort* -- exactly the esr 0x96000010 in the panic.  ABL has just run
   this controller for fastboot, so its clocks are already up; going straight to
   DWC3 is both the safer and the intended state.  sweetusbgcc=1 restores the old
   probing for anyone who wants the register values printed.

Harness: applied=106 skipped=0.
--- a/bsd/dev/fuxi_ufs.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/fuxi_ufs.c
